Resilience patterns protect the **caller** and the **dependency**. Misused, they make outages worse.

## Timeouts

Set explicit timeouts on every outbound call (connect and total). Choose them from the dependency's observed P99 and the page's latency budget; remember that timeouts add up across layers (browser, BFF, CMS). With no timeout, slow responses pile up and exhaust connections or threads.

## Retries

- Retry only **idempotent** requests (GET) and **transient** errors (network failure, 502/503/504, sometimes 429 honouring `Retry-After`). Do not retry 4xx client errors.
- Use **exponential backoff with jitter** so clients do not retry in sync.
- Keep a **small cap** (for example two or three attempts) and consider a retry budget (a limit on the share of traffic that may be retries).
- Retrying at several layers multiplies attempts: three layers with three tries each can make nine calls per request.

```ts
async function fetchWithRetry(url: string, attempts = 3, timeoutMs = 2000): Promise<Response> {
  for (let i = 0; i < attempts; i++) {
    try {
      const res = await fetch(url, { signal: AbortSignal.timeout(timeoutMs) });
      if (res.status < 500) return res;       // success or non-retryable client error
    } catch { /* network error or timeout: fall through to retry */ }
    const backoff = Math.min(1000 * 2 ** i, 4000) * (0.5 + Math.random() / 2);
    await new Promise(r => setTimeout(r, backoff));
  }
  throw new Error("Content API unavailable");
}
```

## Circuit breakers

After a failure threshold, the breaker **opens** and calls fail fast (or return a fallback) instead of waiting. After a cool-down it lets a few probe requests through (**half-open**) and closes again on success. This gives the dependency room to recover.

## Pair with fallbacks

Serve stale cached content, a static default, or hide a non-critical module. Decide per content type what a safe fallback is.

A naive version fetches in `useEffect`, casts the JSON to a type and shows "Loading...". A more production-minded version addresses **cancellation, validation, state modelling, accessibility and rendering location**.

## 1. A reusable, validated client

```ts
import { z } from "zod";

const VehicleSchema = z.object({
  id: z.string(),
  title: z.string(),
  description: z.string(),
  heroImage: z.object({ url: z.string(), altText: z.string() }),
});
export type Vehicle = z.infer<typeof VehicleSchema>;

export async function fetchVehicle(slug: string, locale: string, signal?: AbortSignal): Promise<Vehicle> {
  const url = `/api/content/vehicles/${encodeURIComponent(slug)}?locale=${encodeURIComponent(locale)}`;
  const res = await fetch(url, { signal });
  if (!res.ok) throw new Error(`Content request failed with status ${res.status}`);
  return VehicleSchema.parse(await res.json()); // runtime check, not a cast
}
```

## 2. A component with explicit states and abort handling

```tsx
import { useEffect, useState } from "react";

type State =
  | { status: "loading" }
  | { status: "error" }
  | { status: "success"; data: Vehicle };

export function VehiclePage({ slug, locale }: { slug: string; locale: string }) {
  const [state, setState] = useState<State>({ status: "loading" });

  useEffect(() => {
    const controller = new AbortController();
    setState({ status: "loading" });

    fetchVehicle(slug, locale, controller.signal)
      .then(data => setState({ status: "success", data }))
      .catch(err => {
        if (err instanceof DOMException && err.name === "AbortError") return; // unmounted or params changed
        console.error(err); // send to monitoring in a real app
        setState({ status: "error" });
      });

    return () => controller.abort();
  }, [slug, locale]);

  if (state.status === "loading") return <p role="status">Loading content...</p>;
  if (state.status === "error") return <p role="alert">Content is temporarily unavailable.</p>;

  const { title, heroImage, description } = state.data;
  return (
    <main>
      <h1>{title}</h1>
      <img src={heroImage.url} alt={heroImage.altText} />
      <p>{description}</p>
    </main>
  );
}
```

## What improved and why

- **Abort and race safety.** Changing `slug` or `locale` aborts the previous request, so a slow earlier response cannot overwrite newer state. Abort errors are expected, not failures.
- **Validation.** Bad data becomes a handled error, not a crash in render.
- **Discriminated state** prevents impossible combinations such as both data and error.
- **Dependencies** include `slug` and `locale`, so the effect re-runs when they change.
- **Accessibility.** `role="status"` and `role="alert"`, and real `alt` text from the content.

## SSR and SEO note

An effect only runs in the browser, so crawlers and the first paint receive an empty shell. For public content pages, fetch on the server instead (for example Next.js server components, or SSG/ISR) and render the HTML with data included. Client-side fetching remains suitable for interactive or personalised parts.

## Beyond hand-rolled effects

A library such as TanStack Query or SWR provides caching, de-duplication, retries and revalidation. Whichever you use, keep the API client in one module instead of scattering `fetch` calls through components.
